Q3. Can we achieve Encapsulation without using
getters and setters?

Interview Ready Answer

Yes.

Getters and setters are optional.

Encapsulation means controlling access to data,
not necessarily exposing getters and setters.

A class may expose only business methods.

Example

class BankAccount{

    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount){

        if(amount > 0)
            balance += amount;
    }

    public void withdraw(double amount){

        if(amount > 0 && amount <= balance)
            balance -= amount;
    }
}

Notice

• No Getter
• No Setter

Still,

Encapsulation is achieved because direct access to
balance is restricted and all modifications happen
through controlled methods.

